---
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import { sourceSnippet } from "../../lib/snippet.ts";

const channelFields = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-channel-fields");
const manifestEntry = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-manifest-entry");
const snapshotFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-snapshot");
const preparationState = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-preparation-state");
const prepareFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-prepare");
const deleteFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-delete-instance");
const preservationTest = sourceSnippet("foundations-workspaces-preservation-test");
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="workspaces"
  description="A workspace is the saved board: which components exist, where they sit, and how they are wired. Watershed persists all three beneath one child map, then the browser runtime reconciles that state without discarding data the local host cannot use."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A workspace is the saved board</h2>
    <p>
      The last two sheets built a board out of parts: a notes panel, a
      counter, a task list, an activity log, and the connections a user drew
      between them. Nothing so far has saved it. Close the tab, and the board
      is gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      A workspace is that board, stored. It holds three things, and it is
      worth naming them plainly before any code:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Which parts exist.</strong> One entry for each component
        instance: its kind, its format version, its config, and a handle to
        its own data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where they sit.</strong> The presentation order the users
        arranged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How they are wired.</strong> The port connections from{" "}
        <a href="/foundations/ports">the last sheet</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All three are shared, collaborative state, the same as the data inside
      the components. Two people arranging one board see each other do it.
      That is the whole point, and it is also where the difficulty starts.
    </p>
    <p>
      A saved board outlives the code that made it. Open one tomorrow and
      this build may not carry the counter any more. Someone may have deleted
      a panel that another connection still names. Two people can each add a
      safe connection and get a cycle when their edits merge. A workspace
      cannot answer those problems by deleting what it fails to understand:
      one client's ignorance would become everybody's data loss.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this sheet does two jobs. It stores the board, and it derives the
      part of the board this client can safely run — while keeping the rest
      exactly as written.
    </p>
    <p>
      All of it is host work. The last two sheets each split in half, one part
      for using components and one for writing them. This sheet has no second
      half: a component never sees the workspace that holds it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One root slot opens the whole workspace</h2>
    <p>
      Those three lists become three channels, and one slot on the document
      root opens all of them. A composed app still has one typed document
      root. Give the workspace one <code>ChildField</code> there, and it owns
      a map of component instances, a sequence for presentation order, and a
      sequence of port connections.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={channelFields} />
    <p>
      The JavaScript and BEAM adapters create those channels while they are
      detached, wire their handles into the workspace map, then attach the
      complete subtree through the root. A peer never needs three magic keys
      at the application root, and component data never shares the workspace's
      control namespace.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The manifest points at data; it doesn't swallow it</h2>
    <p>
      Each manifest value names the stable instance and component version,
      carries its config as JSON, and embeds a handle to that component's own
      child map. The config can vary by component kind. The child map remains
      a separate collaborative structure with one owner.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={manifestEntry} />
    <p>
      Handle discovery walks nested JSON. Writing this record therefore
      attaches the child map before the manifest operation reaches another
      client. A late joiner reads the same entry and resolves the same map.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Stored data and usable data are different lists</h2>
    <p>
      Those three reopening problems — the unknown kind, the repeated layout
      entry, the merged cycle — all arrive as stored data this client cannot
      use. None of them makes the data wrong. They make it unusable{" "}
      <em>here</em>, today, by this build.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>workspace.snapshot</code> keeps the raw values, decodes what it can,
      removes duplicate or dangling layout references from the effective view,
      and asks <code>port_graph.effective</code> for the deterministic acyclic
      graph. Every rejected value comes back as a diagnostic. Nothing gets
      rewritten.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={snapshotFn} />
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2><code>Prepared</code> stops before <code>Ready</code></h2>
    <p>
      Persistence can answer three questions without pretending it ran a
      component: does the catalog know this exact kind and version, does the
      config decode, and does the child map resolve? A successful answer is{" "}
      <code>Prepared</code>. An unsupported version is{" "}
      <code>Unavailable</code>. Bad stored data or config is{" "}
      <code>Failed</code>. A handle still in flight stays{" "}
      <code>Loading</code>.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={preparationState} />
    <SnippetBlock snippet={prepareFn} />
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Prepared is not running.</strong> The JavaScript component
        runtime calls the descriptor's start closure, waits while the instance
        is <code>Starting</code>, and reaches <code>Ready</code> only after
        bootstrap and subscriptions finish. Keeping the names separate makes a
        startup failure impossible to hide behind a resolved handle.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Deletion removes reachability, not history</h2>
    <p>
      One delete touches three channels, so order matters. The adapter removes
      every layout reference, removes every connection that reaches the
      instance, and deletes the manifest key last. Repeating the operation
      finishes a partial attempt instead of failing on already-missing data.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={deleteFn} />
    <p>
      Watershed doesn't have channel garbage collection. The instance map
      stays attached and disappears only from workspace reachability. A caller
      that retained its handle can still read it—the test pins that behavior
      instead of calling the orphan “deleted.”
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={preservationTest} />
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>The runtime watches the workspace, not the view tree</h3>
    <p>
      Persistence still stops at <code>Prepared</code>. The JavaScript runtime
      subscribes to the manifest, layout, and graph; starts, replaces, and stops
      only the affected instances; and routes output events through one graph
      snapshot per trace. Layout and connection edits don't restart healthy
      components. The runtime exposes running values and lifecycle states, but
      it doesn't mount a view—the browser host decides what those values look
      like.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The workspace</span>
    <p>
      One child map owns the manifest, layout, and wiring. Raw collaborative
      data stays intact; the local catalog derives the view it can safely use.
      From here, <a href="/guide">the survey procedure</a> returns to the
      document runtime watershed ships end to end today.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
